#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
snake - Native ckb-next animation.

A self-playing classic snake. The snake spawns small in the middle of
the keyboard, hunts down dots that appear in unoccupied cells, grows
each time it eats one, and speeds up the longer it gets. It dies if it
runs into its own body or off the edge of its playfield. After death it
respawns small.

Copyright (c) 2026 Preston A. Elder <prez@neuromancy.net>.
Licensed under the MIT License -- see the LICENSE file in the project
repository for the full text.

Designed and refined by Preston A. Elder <prez@neuromancy.net>.
Code authored by Claude AI under Preston's direction.

Tested on a Corsair K70. Other ckb-next-supported keyboards should work
but key layouts may differ.

Install:
  Drop this file (executable) into your user-local ckb-next animations
  directory, then have the GUI re-scan its scripts. No sudo, no daemon
  restart, no GUI restart needed:

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/ckb-next/animations
    cp snake ~/.local/share/ckb-next/animations/
    chmod +x ~/.local/share/ckb-next/animations/snake

  Then in ckb-next: Settings -> Animation Scripts -> Re-scan. The
  animation appears in the Lighting tab as "Snake".

  In Properties -> Settings -> Playback enable "Start with mode" so the
  animation runs whenever the mode is active. Snake does NOT use key
  presses (kpmode none) -- the snake plays itself.

How the playfield is built:
  The keyboard isn't a perfect grid, so we infer a 4-connected
  adjacency at start time: for each key we find the nearest neighbor in
  N/S/E/W within a tolerance derived from the median key spacing. The
  snake is then confined to whichever connected component it spawned in
  -- normally the main typing block, since that's the largest.

AI strategy:
  * BFS from the head to the nearest reachable dot, treating mid-body
    cells as obstacles (the tail is passable since it leaves the cell
    on the next move). The first step on the shortest path becomes the
    chosen direction.
  * If no dot is reachable, the snake picks the legal move that
    maximizes reachable empty space (flood fill) -- a cheap way to
    avoid trapping itself in a pocket of body.
  * If no legal move exists, the snake dies.

Speedup as it grows:
  Move period shrinks linearly with length, floored at min_period:
    period = max(min_period, base_period - growth_speedup * (length - 1))
  So a default-tuned snake at length 1 moves every 0.35s; by length
  30 it's down to ~0.21s; by length 59 it hits the 0.06s floor. Tune
  growth_speedup=0 to disable speedup entirely.

Parameters (all tunable in the ckb-next GUI):
  base_period     Seconds per move when the snake is at its initial
                  length (default 0.35, range 0.05-2.0)
  min_period      Floor on move period; protects from unplayable speeds
                  at very long lengths (default 0.06, range 0.02-1.0)
  growth_speedup  Seconds shaved off the move period per added segment
                  (default 0.005, range 0.0-0.05; 0.0 disables speedup)
  initial_length  Length the snake spawns (and respawns) at
                  (default 3, range 1-10)
  dot_ttl         Seconds a dot stays on the board before vanishing
                  (default 8.0, range 1.0-60.0)
  dot_interval    Seconds between dot-spawn attempts
                  (default 2.5, range 0.2-30.0)
  max_dots        Cap on simultaneous dots
                  (default 3, range 1-10)
  head_color      ARGB color of the snake's head
                  (default ffe0ffe0, pale green-white)
  body_color      ARGB color of the body; tail end fades toward black
                  (default ff00c800, solid green)
  dot_color       ARGB color of dots; dots blink in the last 1.5s of
                  their TTL (default ffff2030, red)
"""
import sys
import math
import random
from collections import deque


# === Protocol helpers ======================================================

def _need_encode(c):
    o = ord(c)
    return (o <= 0x2C or o == 0x2F or 0x3A <= o <= 0x40
            or o == 0x5B or o == 0x5D or o >= 0x7F)


def url_encode(s):
    out = []
    for c in s:
        if _need_encode(c):
            out.append("%{:02X}".format(ord(c)))
        else:
            out.append(c)
    return "".join(out)


def url_decode(s):
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(s):
        if s[i] == "%" and i + 2 < len(s):
            try:
                out.append(chr(int(s[i + 1:i + 3], 16)))
                i += 3
                continue
            except ValueError:
                pass
        out.append(s[i])
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def read_words():
    line = sys.stdin.readline()
    if not line:
        return ("", "", "")
    parts = line.strip().split(None, 2)
    while len(parts) < 3:
        parts.append("")
    return (url_decode(parts[0]), url_decode(parts[1]), url_decode(parts[2]))


def emit(s): sys.stdout.write(s)
def emit_line(s): sys.stdout.write(s + "\n")
def flush(): sys.stdout.flush()


